<!-- §01 -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>Clustering covers space. It doesn't follow variance.</h2>
<p>Training a frontier model on the full dataset is wasteful — carefully chosen subsets reach nearly the same accuracy at a fraction of the cost. The question is <em>which</em> subset. The influential coreset view of <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.00489" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sener &amp; Savarese (2018)</a> and the sensitivity-sampling extension of Axiotis et al. (2024) both answer it geometrically: pick points that <strong>cover the data space</strong> — k-center, k-means, farthest-point spreading.</p>
<p>That works when the dataset's structure is a set of compact clusters. But many modern embedding datasets have a different shape: a <strong>global algebraic structure</strong>, where most of the signal lives along a few dominant directions of variance and the rest is near-noise. On data like that, "cover the space" spends precious budget on the corners and outliers — exactly the low-variance directions that carry the least training signal.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head"><span class="vp-title">selection_strategy.viz</span><span class="vp-hint">toggle the two strategies</span></div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="cloud-canvas" height="360"></canvas>
    <div class="vp-controls">
      <button class="vp-btn active" data-mode="lowrank">Low-rank (spectral)</button>
      <button class="vp-btn" data-mode="cluster">Clustering (diversity)</button>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-cap" id="cloud-cap"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Same elongated cloud, same budget of 8 points. <strong>Clustering</strong> fans out to the corners — it grabs off-axis outliers because they maximize spread. <strong>Low-rank</strong> instead picks the extremes <em>along the dominant axis</em>: the points that best reconstruct the dataset's principal direction of variance. When the loss is governed by that direction, those are the points that matter.</p>

<div class="callout">
<b>The one-line claim.</b> Replace "select for diversity" with "select for spectral leverage." A weighted subset of just <b>Õ(k + 1/ε²)</b> points approximates the full average loss to a (1±ε) factor, plus an additive term that shrinks with Φ<sub>k</sub> — the optimal rank-<i>k</i> approximation error of the embedding matrix.
</div>
</section>

<!-- §02 -->
<section id="s-coreset" data-code="coreset">
<div class="section-no">02 — What a Coreset Must Do</div>
<h2>Approximate the loss, not the shape</h2>
<p>The object we want to preserve isn't the geometry of the points — it's the <strong>sum of losses</strong>. A coreset <em>S</em> with weights <em>w</em> is good if the weighted loss over <em>S</em> matches the total loss over the full dataset <em>D</em>. The gap is the quantity the algorithm directly minimizes:</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">coreset loss — the thing we minimize</div>
Δ(<span class="math-var">S</span>) = | Σ<sub>x∈D</sub> ℓ(x) − Σ<sub>x∈S</sub> <span class="math-var">w(x)</span> ℓ(x) |
<span class="math-comment">  // full total loss vs. weighted subset estimate</span>
</div>

<p>Why does matching the loss license you to <em>train</em> on the subset? Because the test error decomposes into three terms, and this is the only one the selection algorithm controls:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Generalization error</strong> — empirical vs. population loss (a property of the data, not the subset).</li>
  <li><strong>Training error</strong> — how well the model fits whatever it's given.</li>
  <li><strong>Coreset loss</strong> — how faithfully <em>S</em> reproduces <em>D</em>'s loss. ← this is Δ(<em>S</em>).</li>
</ul>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head"><span class="vp-title">coreset_loss.viz</span><span class="vp-hint">n weighted points reproduce the full sum</span></div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="coreset-canvas" height="240"></canvas>
    <div class="vp-cap">Each selected point is re-weighted by 1/(s·p(x)) so its contribution stands in for many unselected points — the two loss gauges stay matched even though the right side holds far fewer points.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Crucially, the paper scores points by the <strong>current</strong> model's loss, not the loss after retraining. If a weighted subset reproduces the current loss well, it contains representative points along the dominant directions — and training on it then tracks training on all of <em>D</em>, with no assumption about labels or reaching zero training loss.</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 -->
<section id="s-lowrank" data-code="lowrank">
<div class="section-no">03 — Low-Rank Structure &amp; k</div>
<h2>The knob: how many directions to keep</h2>
<p>"Low-rank structure" has a precise meaning. Take the embedding matrix <em>D</em> and its singular value decomposition <em>D = UΣVᵀ</em>. The singular values σ<sub>1</sub> ≥ σ<sub>2</sub> ≥ … measure how much variance lives along each direction. Keeping the top <em>k</em> gives the best rank-<em>k</em> approximation; everything you throw away is the residual energy:</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">rank-k approximation error (Eckart–Young–Mirsky)</div>
Φ<sub>k</sub>(<span class="math-var">D</span>) = min<sub>rank(B)≤k</sub> ‖<span class="math-var">D</span> − B‖²<sub>F</sub> = Σ<sub>i&gt;k</sub> σ<sub>i</sub>²
<span class="math-comment">  // the energy in the discarded tail directions</span>
</div>

<p>This Φ<sub>k</sub> is exactly the additive term in the coreset guarantee. A <strong>nearly low-rank</strong> dataset (sharp singular-value decay) has tiny Φ<sub>k</sub>, so the coreset is nearly lossless. A high-rank, noisy dataset has large Φ<sub>k</sub> — the same fundamental hardness clustering faces with outliers, made explicit.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head"><span class="vp-title">singular_spectrum.viz</span><span class="vp-hint">drag k — watch Φₖ shrink</span></div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="svd-canvas" height="280"></canvas>
    <div class="vp-slider">
      <span>k = <b id="k-val" style="color:var(--purple-l)">3</b></span>
      <input type="range" id="k-slider" min="1" max="8" value="3" step="1">
      <span id="k-stats" style="white-space:nowrap"></span>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-cap">Purple bars are the kept directions; grey bars are the discarded tail whose squared sum is Φ<sub>k</sub>. The paper sets k ≈ 20–25% of the dataset as landmark points.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>In the experiments, <em>k</em> isn't a hyperparameter you sweep blindly — it's the number of <strong>landmark points</strong> whose true loss you actually compute (the expensive forward passes). Everything else is reconstructed from cheap embeddings. So <em>k</em> is simultaneously the rank of the sketch and the labeling budget.</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 -->
<section id="s-sensitivity" data-code="sensitivity">
<div class="section-no">04 — Leverage Scores &amp; the Algorithm</div>
<h2>Decompose each point, then sample by importance</h2>
<p>Here's the mechanism. Project the subspace <em>V</em> spanned by the landmark directions. Every point <em>x</em> splits into two pieces: its <strong>projection</strong> <em>v(x)</em> inside <em>V</em>, and its <strong>residual</strong> <em>r(x)</em> orthogonal to <em>V</em>. The residual is the part the low-rank sketch can't explain.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head"><span class="vp-title">residual_decomposition.viz</span><span class="vp-hint">hover/click a point to decompose it</span></div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="decomp-canvas" height="340"></canvas>
    <div class="vp-cap" id="decomp-cap">Each point is colored by its sensitivity score σ(x). Brighter purple = more likely to be sampled. Click any point to see its projection v(x) (cyan) and residual r(x) (amber).</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The sensitivity score blends both pieces: the loss explained along the kept directions, plus a penalty λ‖r(x)‖² for the unexplained residual. Sample proportionally to it, then weight inversely to keep the estimator unbiased:</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">sensitivity score for point x (Algorithm 1)</div>
σ(<span class="math-var">x</span>) = (γ+1)·(α₁²ℓ(v₁) + … + α<sub>t</sub>²ℓ(v<sub>t</sub>)) + γkξ + λ·‖<span class="math-var">r(x)</span>‖²
<span class="math-comment">  // projected-loss term  +  basis term  +  residual penalty</span>
p(<span class="math-var">x</span>) = σ(<span class="math-var">x</span>) / Σ<sub>y</sub> σ(y)      w(<span class="math-var">x</span>) = 1 / (s·p(<span class="math-var">x</span>))
</div>

<div class="step-list" id="alg-steps">
  <div class="step-box"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-body"><div class="step-title">Pick landmarks → subspace V</div><div class="step-desc">Choose k rows via leverage-score sampling; span them to form V.</div><div class="step-code">C ⊆ D ; V = span(C)</div></div></div>
  <div class="step-box"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-body"><div class="step-title">Compute residuals</div><div class="step-desc">For every point, the part orthogonal to V.</div><div class="step-code">r(x) = x − Proj(x, V)</div></div></div>
  <div class="step-box"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-body"><div class="step-title">Score sensitivity</div><div class="step-desc">Projected loss + residual penalty.</div><div class="step-code">σ(x) = (γ+1)Σαᵢ²ℓ(vᵢ) + γkξ + λ‖r(x)‖²</div></div></div>
  <div class="step-box"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-body"><div class="step-title">Normalize to probabilities</div><div class="step-desc">Turn scores into a sampling distribution.</div><div class="step-code">p(x) = σ(x) / Σ σ(y)</div></div></div>
  <div class="step-box"><div class="step-num">5</div><div class="step-body"><div class="step-title">Sample s points, weight them</div><div class="step-desc">Inverse-probability weights → unbiased loss estimate.</div><div class="step-code">w(x) = 1 / (s·p(x))</div></div></div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="step-prev">‹ Prev</button>
  <span class="step-prog" id="step-prog">Step 1 of 5</span>
  <button id="step-next">Next ›</button>
</div>

<p>Only step 1 and the landmark losses require real model evaluations — k ≈ 20% of the data. Everything else runs on cheap BERT embeddings. Counting a backward pass as 2× a forward pass, selection costs roughly <strong>6.7%</strong> of a full training run.</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 -->
<section id="s-tradeoff" data-code="tradeoff">
<div class="section-no">05 — The Error vs. k Trade-off</div>
<h2>Low-rank error stays below clustering at every k</h2>
<p>Is GSM8k actually more low-rank than clustered? The paper tests it directly: for a range of <em>k</em>, compare the average reconstruction error from <em>k</em> cluster centers against the rank-<em>k</em> low-rank approximation, and likewise compare how well each reproduces per-sample loss. Low-rank wins on both, at every <em>k</em>.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head"><span class="vp-title">error_vs_k.viz</span><span class="vp-hint">switch the two error metrics</span></div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="error-canvas" height="300"></canvas>
    <div class="vp-controls">
      <button class="vp-btn active" data-err="proj">Projection / clustering error</button>
      <button class="vp-btn" data-err="loss">Loss approximation error</button>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-cap" id="error-cap"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The same picture holds on the classic <strong>Credit Card Default</strong> tabular benchmark (30k rows, 23 features). At a budget of s=1000, low-rank sensitivity sampling cuts the coreset approximation error by an <strong>order of magnitude</strong> versus uniform sampling, and by <strong>~50%</strong> versus clustering — with all methods converging as the budget grows.</p>

<div class="callout">
<b>Where the knob bites.</b> One subtlety: a large residual weight λ makes the score dominated by geometric distance r, so it starts chasing outliers — and for clustering, adding <i>more</i> clusters then makes it <i>worse</i> (finer partitions shrink r for inliers, biasing selection toward the noise). Low-rank sampling is markedly more robust to λ, which is why it holds up across settings.
</div>
</section>

<!-- §06 -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results</div>
<h2>Better coresets → better fine-tuned models</h2>
<p>The payoff is end-to-end fine-tuning accuracy. On <strong>Llama3-8B</strong> across GSM8k, ViGGO, and SQL generation (BERT embeddings, k = 25% landmarks), low-rank sensitivity sampling beats uniform sampling consistently and edges out clustering-based sensitivity sampling on average. The sharpest single contrast is ViGGO at a 25% data budget:</p>

