<p>On the Credit Card benchmark, logistic regression trained on low-rank coresets reaches <strong>74%</strong> test accuracy at s=5000, versus ~70% for clustering coresets and ~67% for random sampling. The lesson is consistent: when a dataset's signal lives along a few dominant directions — as embedding datasets so often do — selecting for <strong>spectral leverage</strong> beats selecting for diversity, with a clean theoretical guarantee tied to Φ<sub>k</sub> and a selection cost under 7% of a training run.</p>

<div class="callout">
<b>Takeaway.</b> Coreset selection has long been framed geometrically — "cover the space." This paper shows the more useful question is algebraic — "preserve the dominant directions of variance." Same budget, better data, a guarantee that degrades gracefully exactly when the data is hard (large Φ<sub>k</sub>).
</div>
</section>

<!-- Listen-friendly content -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display: none;" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: Low-Rank Data Selection</h2>
    <p>Training large models on full datasets is wasteful, so researchers select small representative subsets called coresets. The dominant prior approach, going back to Sener and Savarese, picks points geometrically — clustering or farthest-point sampling to cover the data space. This paper argues that for modern embedding datasets, which carry their signal along a few dominant directions of variance, covering the space wastes budget on low-variance outliers.</p>
    <p>Instead, the authors select data using low-rank structure. They build a low-rank sketch of the dataset via singular value decomposition, decompose each point into a projection inside the top subspace and a residual orthogonal to it, and assign each point a sensitivity score combining its projected loss with a penalty on the residual. Sampling proportional to that score and weighting inversely gives an unbiased estimate of the total loss. The theory guarantees a weighted subset of order k plus one over epsilon squared points approximates the average loss within a one plus epsilon factor, plus an additive term equal to the optimal rank-k approximation error, Phi-k.</p>
    <p>Empirically, on the Credit Card Default tabular dataset, low-rank sensitivity sampling cuts coreset error by an order of magnitude over random and about fifty percent over clustering. Fine-tuning Llama3-8B on GSM8k, ViGGO, and SQL generation, it beats uniform sampling and edges out clustering-based sensitivity sampling on average, while costing under seven percent of a full training run to run the selection.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：低秩数据选择</h2>
    <p>用完整数据集训练大模型代价高昂，因此研究者会挑选一个小而有代表性的子集，称为核心集。以往主流方法源自 Sener 和 Savarese，从几何角度挑点——用聚类或最远点采样去覆盖整个数据空间。本文指出，对于现代的嵌入数据集，其信号往往集中在少数几个主方差方向上，单纯覆盖空间会把预算浪费在低方差的离群点上。</p>
    <p>作者改用低秩结构来选择数据。他们通过奇异值分解构建数据集的低秩草图，把每个点分解为落在主子空间内的投影部分，以及与之正交的残差部分，并为每个点计算一个敏感度分数，将投影损失与残差惩罚结合起来。按该分数成比例采样、再做反比加权，就能得到对总损失的无偏估计。理论保证：规模约为 k 加上一除以 epsilon 平方的加权子集，可在 1 加 epsilon 的因子内逼近平均损失，外加一个等于最优秩 k 近似误差 Phi-k 的可加项。</p>
    <p>实验方面，在信用卡违约表格数据集上，低秩敏感度采样将核心集误差相比随机采样降低一个数量级，相比聚类约降低一半。在 GSM8k、ViGGO 和 SQL 生成任务上微调 Llama3-8B 时，它优于均匀采样，平均也略胜基于聚类的敏感度采样，而整个选择过程的开销不到完整训练的百分之七。</p>
  </div>
</div>

</article>

